<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=greek-alphabet">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greek alphabet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Greek alphabet is the script of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a>, in use since the late 9th or early 8th century BCE (conventionally about 800 BCE), with early samples from at least 775 BCE. Adapted from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a>, it turned some Phoenician consonant letters into vowels and so became the earliest known alphabet to write vowels as well as consonants systematically. Local variants gave way by the end of the 4th century BCE to the 24-letter Ionian alphabet, still used today; its western form was the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet.jpg" alt="Greek alphabet" width="320" height="114" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Maunde Thompson (1840–1929) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bankes_Homer_-_2nd_Century..JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 800 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Greece</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The oldest substantial inscriptions date from about 740 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet<span>c. 1050 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=greek-alphabet">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
